WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
A bus needs a route through a changing city.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
A digital twin uses observations to explore the options.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
The route is calculated before the bus reaches the junction.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.000
But the answer relies on an older picture of the road.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:32.000
The answer arrived on time. The world had changed.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:39.000
What if we update every digital state immediately?

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:46.000
At scale, those updates compete for limited bandwidth.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Sending everything can leave important changes waiting.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Which update matters most to this decision?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
The oldest information is not always the most relevant.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Freshness must be considered together with the task.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Fresh information still needs computation.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:28.000
The nearby edge may be busy. The cloud needs communication.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Faster processing can also compete with an energy budget.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.000
The research challenge: schedule execution and state updates together.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Existing methods face different objectives and constraints.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:56.000
A method that fits one problem may not fit the next.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Designing a new algorithm still demands expert effort.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Can we reuse scheduling knowledge instead of starting over?

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:17.000
I explore AI-assisted generation of candidate algorithms.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Candidates must satisfy constraints and be evaluated.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:31.000
The aim is transferable design knowledge, not just generated code.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Next: investigate adaptation as runtime conditions change.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:45.000
The goal: timely, relevant information for a changing world.